Attribution notes

Project ELIZA is the forensic investigation suite: the layer that keeps an
investigation's claims tied to the documents they rest on, and the gate that
decides what may leave the machine. It is NOT the therapeutic agent in this
tree -- that is a SEPARATE project, Project Nurse Ratchet, with its own package,
its own release gate and its own citations. The two are not variants of one
another and must not be described as one project.

The name ELIZA refers to Joseph Weizenbaum's 1966 program (Communications of the
ACM, 9(1):36-45). No code from that program is included here. The reference is
to the cautionary lesson about a system that is trusted because it appears to be
doing work that nothing underneath is doing -- the exact failure mode an
investigation toolchain exists to make visible.

What is held back, and where that rule lives

`PUBLIC_MANIFEST.json` is an allowlist, not a deny-list: nothing ships that it
does not name, and `tools/eliza_release_gate.py` fails the build when a path
crosses the line the manifest declares. Its `never_ship` and `deny_substrings`
sections name the material that stays home -- raw primary documents,
entity-resolution output, rendered findings, and correspondence naming
identified organisations and people. `DISCLOSURE_POLICY.md` states that line in
prose; the manifest enforces it mechanically.

No primary document about an identified person is distributed with this
project. The investigation METHOD is published; the allegations inside the
original case are not, because no licence makes republishing mental-health,
tenancy or employment records about named people lawful or decent. A reader
verifies a claim by re-fetching the source and comparing its SHA-256, which is
why every instrument here runs against data the reader supplies rather than
against a bundled case corpus.

No third-party runtime dependency is bundled. The Contributor Covenant text in
CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md is used under CC BY 4.0 with attribution retained in the
Attribution section at the end of that file.
